<activity><title>Making a Choice</title>

<introduction><p>Hick's Law says that the time, <m>T</m>, required for a person to make a choice from a list or menu of <m>n</m> items is proportional to <m>\log_2 {n+1}</m>. A classic study measured the amount of time shoppers took to choose a flavor of jam from a display of 6 flavors and from a display of 24 flavors. Hick's Law is used by restaurants and web-designers in making menus, and by survey companies in designing multiple-choice questionaires.</p></introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>Graciela is choosing an AirBnB for her next vacation. If there are <m>n</m> choices available, it will take her <m>T</m> minutes to make her choice, where
      <md>T=5 \log_2 {n+1}</md> 
Complete the table and make a graph.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>2(a) Although <m>n=0</m> does not make sense for the application, point out that we can add that point to complete the graph.</p></aside>

        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>n</m></cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>7</cell>
                    <cell>15</cell>
                    <cell>31</cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>T</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular>

            <image source="act-10-1-7.png" width="60%"><description>grid 0 to 36 by 0 to 36</description></image></li>

<li><p>If Graciela has 30 minutes to spend choosing an AirBnB, how many can she consider?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>A Log Function</title>

<introduction><p>In this Activity we compare the graphs of <m>~y=\sqrt{x}~</m>  and <m>~y=\log_2{x}~</m>.</p></introduction>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>The goal of Activity 3 is to demonstrate that logarithmic functions grow very slowly, just as exponential functions grow rapidly.</p></aside>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>Use your calculator to complete the table. Use the formula <m>~\log_2{x} = \dfrac{\log{x}}{\log{2}}</m> when needed to evaluate <m>\log_2{x}</m>. Round values to tenths.</p>

<sidebyside widths="40% 55%" valigns="middle middle">
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\sqrt{x}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\log_2{x}</m></cell></row>
                <row>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>1</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>2</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>4</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>6</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>8</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>12</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>16</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>20</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>32</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular>
        <image source="act-10-1-8.png"><description>grid 0 to 20 by -2 to 5</description></image></sidebyside></li>

<li><p>Plot both functions on the grid.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>3(b) The graphs are very close; students will have to be precise in their work. After they have finished their graphs, it might be instructive to display a large version using Desmos or some other graphing facility.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>At which two points do the graphs intersect?</p>
    <p>Which graph has larger function values in the long run?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Logarithmic Equations</title>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>What is a log equation?</p>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>4(b) We solve simple log equations by converting to exponential form. You may need to review the equivalence with some numerical examples.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>Follow the steps to solve the equation <m>~\log_2 {(3x-1)} = 5</m></p>
    <p><m>\blert{\text{Convert to exponential form.}}</m></p>
    <p><m>\blert{\text{Solve the new equation.}}</m></p>
    <p><m>\blert{\text{Check the solution.}}</m></p></li>

<li><p> To solve more complicated log equations, we first use the log properties to combine any expressions 
containing logs into a single logarithm.</p>

           <aside component="instructor"><p>4(c) Stress that the first step is to combine the logs into one log, using the log properties.</p></aside>

<p>Follow the steps to solve <m>~~\log_4 {(x+8)} + \log_4 {(x+2)} = 2</m></p>
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Use log properties to write the left side as a single log.}}</m></p> 
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Convert the equation to exponential form.}}</m></p>
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Solve the new equation.}}</m></p>
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Don't forget to check for extraneous solutions!}}</m></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>If you double the number of choices under Hick's Law in Activity 1, approximately how much more time will you need to make your choice?</p></li>
    <li><p>Suppose you don't have a calculator handy. How can you estimate the value of <m>\log_2 {50}</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>How do we check for extraneous solutions when solving a logarithmic equation?</p></li>
    <li><p>How might you use the properties of logarithms when solving a logarithmic equation?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>If you have a formula for a function, how might you find a formula for its inverse?</p></li>
    <li><p>Describe the relationship between the graph of a function and the graph of its inverse.</p></li>
    <li><p>Why does the graph of a log function appear only to the right of the <m>y</m>-axis?</p></li>
    <li><p>Why does the graph of <m>~y = \log_b x~</m> have an <m>x</m>-intercept at <m>(1,0)</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Which function is its own inverse?</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=x^2</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^3</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=2^x</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What is the first step in solving  <m>~\log {x} + \log{x-1} = 2</m>?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><p><m>\log{2x-1}=2</p></m></li>
        <li><p><m>\log{x} = 2 - \log{x-1}</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>\log{x(x-1)} = 2</m></p></li>
        <li><p<m>x + x-1 = 2^x</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What is the <m>y</m>-intercept of the graph of <m>f(x) = \log_5{x}</m> ?</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>(0,1)</m></li>
        <li><m>(1,0)</m></li>
        <li><m>(0,5)</m></li>
        <li>There is none.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>If <m>F(x) = \log_2{x}</m>, then <m>F^{-1}(x)</m> is</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{\log_2{x}}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=2^x</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{2x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\log{\dfrac{1}{2x}}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>